1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,448
On a Thursday in November,

2
00:00:02,448 --> 00:00:02,896
June,

3
00:00:02,896 --> 00:00:04,287
a nurse from Ohio,

4
00:00:04,287 --> 00:00:06,644
got what she'd asked her brokerage app

5
00:00:06,644 --> 00:00:07,450
for.

6
00:00:07,450 --> 00:00:10,167
One share of the most talked-about company

7
00:00:10,167 --> 00:00:11,050
on Wall Street,

8
00:00:11,050 --> 00:00:13,087
at the offer price of eighty-three

9
00:00:13,087 --> 00:00:14,140
dollars.

10
00:00:14,150 --> 00:00:15,332
By lunchtime,

11
00:00:15,332 --> 00:00:17,690
it was worth a lot more.

12
00:00:17,690 --> 00:00:19,069
By the closing bell,

13
00:00:19,069 --> 00:00:20,300
a bit less.

14
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:22,290
But here's what puzzled her.

15
00:00:22,370 --> 00:00:24,638
The company behind that share is barely

16
00:00:24,638 --> 00:00:26,750
old enough for kindergarten.

17
00:00:26,850 --> 00:00:28,450
It doesn't make chips.

18
00:00:28,450 --> 00:00:30,580
It doesn't own a giant cloud.

19
00:00:30,580 --> 00:00:32,813
And its biggest partners are also its

20
00:00:32,813 --> 00:00:33,907
shareholders,

21
00:00:33,907 --> 00:00:36,010
its suppliers and its rivals.

22
00:00:36,010 --> 00:00:38,994
So what exactly did June just buy?

23
00:00:54,050 --> 00:00:56,080
Here's how the day went.

24
00:00:56,250 --> 00:00:58,025
The shares went to the first buyers

25
00:00:58,025 --> 00:00:59,112
the night before,

26
00:00:59,112 --> 00:01:00,550
at June's price.

27
00:01:00,550 --> 00:01:02,925
When trading opened on the Nasdaq,

28
00:01:02,925 --> 00:01:04,429
under the ticker A,

29
00:01:04,429 --> 00:01:04,843
N,

30
00:01:04,843 --> 00:01:05,257
T,

31
00:01:05,257 --> 00:01:07,600
buyers rushed in and the price leapt.

32
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:09,733
Then the excitement cooled,

33
00:01:09,733 --> 00:01:12,070
and early buyers took quick profits.

34
00:01:12,090 --> 00:01:12,942
By the close,

35
00:01:12,942 --> 00:01:15,343
one share was worth about ninety-seven

36
00:01:15,343 --> 00:01:16,380
dollars.

37
00:01:16,390 --> 00:01:18,340
Still a gain for June.

38
00:01:18,340 --> 00:01:20,070
Just a smaller one.

39
00:01:20,317 --> 00:01:20,906
Now,

40
00:01:20,906 --> 00:01:23,057
the headline number.

41
00:01:23,247 --> 00:01:25,859
You'll see this company valued at several

42
00:01:25,859 --> 00:01:26,491
prices,

43
00:01:26,491 --> 00:01:28,397
and they can all be right.

44
00:01:28,647 --> 00:01:30,367
Let me draw why.

45
00:01:30,467 --> 00:01:32,585
Share price times the shares that exist

46
00:01:32,585 --> 00:01:34,667
today gives one number.

47
00:01:34,797 --> 00:01:37,237
Add every share that could exist soon,

48
00:01:37,237 --> 00:01:38,558
from employee options,

49
00:01:38,558 --> 00:01:40,796
stock awards and notes that turn into

50
00:01:40,796 --> 00:01:41,222
stock,

51
00:01:41,222 --> 00:01:42,990
and you get a bigger one,

52
00:01:42,990 --> 00:01:45,157
called fully diluted.

53
00:01:45,367 --> 00:01:46,448
At June's price,

54
00:01:46,448 --> 00:01:47,749
fully diluted,

55
00:01:47,749 --> 00:01:49,734
Wall Street called it about one and

56
00:01:49,734 --> 00:01:51,657
a half trillion dollars.

57
00:01:51,687 --> 00:01:53,087
Same company.

58
00:01:53,147 --> 00:01:54,628
Different counting.

59
00:01:54,883 --> 00:01:57,184
The sale itself was the biggest stock

60
00:01:57,184 --> 00:01:58,772
offering on record,

61
00:01:58,772 --> 00:02:01,123
about eighty-one billion dollars.

62
00:02:01,123 --> 00:02:03,017
And almost all of that money went

63
00:02:03,017 --> 00:02:04,258
to the company,

64
00:02:04,258 --> 00:02:05,290
as new shares,

65
00:02:05,290 --> 00:02:07,573
not to insiders cashing out.

66
00:02:07,803 --> 00:02:10,723
The co-founders each sold only a sliver.

67
00:02:10,853 --> 00:02:11,240
Then,

68
00:02:11,240 --> 00:02:12,647
right after the sale,

69
00:02:12,647 --> 00:02:14,074
a group of chip and cloud giants

70
00:02:14,074 --> 00:02:15,888
agreed to buy even more stock,

71
00:02:15,888 --> 00:02:17,073
at the same price.

72
00:02:17,073 --> 00:02:18,719
Remember that group.

73
00:02:23,183 --> 00:02:25,983
Rewind to the beginning.

74
00:02:26,383 --> 00:02:28,590
A group of researchers left OpenAI,

75
00:02:28,590 --> 00:02:30,190
the company behind ChatGPT,

76
00:02:30,190 --> 00:02:31,603
to start their own lab.

77
00:02:31,603 --> 00:02:32,656
Among them,

78
00:02:32,656 --> 00:02:34,601
a brother and sister:

79
00:02:34,601 --> 00:02:36,195
Dario Amodei,

80
00:02:36,195 --> 00:02:38,599
who became chief executive,

81
00:02:38,599 --> 00:02:40,733
and Daniela Amodei,

82
00:02:40,733 --> 00:02:42,143
president.

83
00:02:42,283 --> 00:02:44,418
They made Anthropic a public benefit

84
00:02:44,418 --> 00:02:45,369
corporation,

85
00:02:45,369 --> 00:02:47,505
allowed to weigh its mission alongside

86
00:02:47,505 --> 00:02:48,493
profit.

87
00:02:48,633 --> 00:02:51,446
Their product was an AI assistant called

88
00:02:51,446 --> 00:02:52,302
Claude.

89
00:02:52,550 --> 00:02:53,325
At first,

90
00:02:53,325 --> 00:02:56,250
Claude was one chatbot among many.

91
00:02:56,250 --> 00:02:58,090
Then it found its niche.

92
00:02:58,130 --> 00:03:00,110
Programmers used it to write code,

93
00:03:00,110 --> 00:03:02,310
and companies plugged it into their own

94
00:03:02,310 --> 00:03:03,310
products.

95
00:03:03,430 --> 00:03:05,339
And most of the money doesn't come

96
00:03:05,339 --> 00:03:07,260
from monthly subscriptions.

97
00:03:07,260 --> 00:03:09,220
It comes from usage.

98
00:03:09,270 --> 00:03:11,422
Businesses pay for the work Claude does,

99
00:03:11,422 --> 00:03:14,194
a little like an electricity bill.

100
00:03:14,450 --> 00:03:16,855
And that bill grew at a speed

101
00:03:16,855 --> 00:03:19,860
Wall Street had rarely seen.

102
00:03:19,910 --> 00:03:22,170
When Claude first went on sale,

103
00:03:22,170 --> 00:03:24,200
revenue was tiny.

104
00:03:24,370 --> 00:03:25,631
So far this year,

105
00:03:25,631 --> 00:03:26,891
through September,

106
00:03:26,891 --> 00:03:29,464
Anthropic booked thirty-three billion

107
00:03:29,464 --> 00:03:31,350
dollars in sales.

108
00:03:31,670 --> 00:03:33,788
That's the rocket the whole price is

109
00:03:33,788 --> 00:03:35,093
strapped to.

110
00:03:35,350 --> 00:03:38,370
But growth is only one scoreboard.

111
00:03:38,370 --> 00:03:40,090
Let me draw the others.

112
00:03:40,090 --> 00:03:42,590
Revenue is what customers paid.

113
00:03:42,590 --> 00:03:44,522
The run rate is this month's pace,

114
00:03:44,522 --> 00:03:46,590
stretched over a year.

115
00:03:46,750 --> 00:03:48,782
Operating profit is what's left after

116
00:03:48,782 --> 00:03:50,490
running the business.

117
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:52,944
Net profit counts everything else,

118
00:03:52,944 --> 00:03:54,672
and this year it shows a giant

119
00:03:54,672 --> 00:03:55,141
loss,

120
00:03:55,141 --> 00:03:58,103
mostly accounting on notes that turn into

121
00:03:58,103 --> 00:03:58,950
stock.

122
00:03:58,980 --> 00:03:59,834
And cash flow,

123
00:03:59,834 --> 00:04:02,002
the money that actually came in,

124
00:04:02,002 --> 00:04:03,697
turned positive.

125
00:04:08,150 --> 00:04:10,470
So where does the money go?

126
00:04:10,510 --> 00:04:13,700
Follow June's share into a data center.

127
00:04:13,880 --> 00:04:16,880
Every answer Claude writes runs on rooms

128
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:20,126
full of specialized chips that need power,

129
00:04:20,126 --> 00:04:22,090
cooling and buildings.

130
00:04:22,260 --> 00:04:24,940
Training a new model costs a fortune.

131
00:04:24,940 --> 00:04:27,498
Running it costs money every time someone

132
00:04:27,498 --> 00:04:28,950
asks a question.

133
00:04:29,130 --> 00:04:30,588
That's called inference,

134
00:04:30,588 --> 00:04:33,300
and it grows with every customer.

135
00:04:33,550 --> 00:04:35,820
Here's the key ratio.

136
00:04:35,860 --> 00:04:36,531
Last year,

137
00:04:36,531 --> 00:04:38,822
for every dollar of sales,

138
00:04:38,822 --> 00:04:41,599
Anthropic spent about a dollar sixty on

139
00:04:41,599 --> 00:04:44,050
computing and infrastructure.

140
00:04:44,180 --> 00:04:46,177
It was selling dollars that cost more

141
00:04:46,177 --> 00:04:48,109
than a dollar to make.

142
00:04:48,350 --> 00:04:49,073
This year,

143
00:04:49,073 --> 00:04:50,920
the picture flipped.

144
00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:53,152
For every dollar of sales,

145
00:04:53,152 --> 00:04:56,010
computing cost about fifty-seven cents.

146
00:04:56,030 --> 00:04:57,382
Bigger customers,

147
00:04:57,382 --> 00:04:58,963
more efficient models,

148
00:04:58,963 --> 00:05:00,480
better chip deals.

149
00:05:00,570 --> 00:05:03,260
That's the chart the optimists point to.

150
00:05:03,517 --> 00:05:06,208
And here's the chart the skeptics point

151
00:05:06,208 --> 00:05:07,107
to.

152
00:05:07,187 --> 00:05:08,401
To keep growing,

153
00:05:08,401 --> 00:05:11,126
Anthropic has signed contracts for future

154
00:05:11,126 --> 00:05:12,588
computing power,

155
00:05:12,588 --> 00:05:14,298
data centers and chips,

156
00:05:14,298 --> 00:05:16,999
worth about nine hundred billion dollars,

157
00:05:16,999 --> 00:05:18,460
stretching over years,

158
00:05:18,460 --> 00:05:20,547
some over decades.

159
00:05:20,617 --> 00:05:23,017
That's not money already spent.

160
00:05:23,037 --> 00:05:25,068
But much of it is promised,

161
00:05:25,068 --> 00:05:27,949
whether the customers show up or not.

162
00:05:28,183 --> 00:05:30,230
Let me separate some words that get

163
00:05:30,230 --> 00:05:31,423
mixed up.

164
00:05:31,423 --> 00:05:33,773
A commitment is a promise to buy

165
00:05:33,773 --> 00:05:34,843
later.

166
00:05:34,933 --> 00:05:37,643
Spending is cash going out.

167
00:05:37,643 --> 00:05:40,176
Debt is borrowed money that must be

168
00:05:40,176 --> 00:05:41,283
repaid.

169
00:05:41,323 --> 00:05:43,761
An expense hits profit as the service

170
00:05:43,761 --> 00:05:45,093
gets used.

171
00:05:45,193 --> 00:05:47,954
Anthropic's giant number is mostly the

172
00:05:47,954 --> 00:05:48,754
first kind,

173
00:05:48,754 --> 00:05:50,963
and its actual debt is small next

174
00:05:50,963 --> 00:05:52,943
to its cash pile.

175
00:05:53,183 --> 00:05:55,333
The risk isn't a loan.

176
00:05:55,333 --> 00:05:56,968
It's the promise.

177
00:05:57,217 --> 00:05:59,257
So the bet comes down to one

178
00:05:59,257 --> 00:06:00,367
question.

179
00:06:00,577 --> 00:06:03,602
Can Anthropic earn more from every dollar

180
00:06:03,602 --> 00:06:05,594
of computing than it costs,

181
00:06:05,594 --> 00:06:07,707
while those bills come due?

182
00:06:08,197 --> 00:06:09,855
If demand keeps climbing,

183
00:06:09,855 --> 00:06:12,137
the promises will look cheap.

184
00:06:12,317 --> 00:06:13,365
If it stalls,

185
00:06:13,365 --> 00:06:15,307
they'll look enormous.

186
00:06:19,750 --> 00:06:22,819
Now meet the strangest relationship in

187
00:06:22,819 --> 00:06:24,340
this story.

188
00:06:24,550 --> 00:06:25,710
Amazon.

189
00:06:25,810 --> 00:06:28,477
It has invested billions in Anthropic

190
00:06:28,477 --> 00:06:30,290
since its early days.

191
00:06:30,320 --> 00:06:32,596
Its cloud rents Anthropic much of its

192
00:06:32,596 --> 00:06:34,088
computing power,

193
00:06:34,088 --> 00:06:36,870
and Anthropic has promised to spend at

194
00:06:36,870 --> 00:06:39,400
least a hundred and ten billion dollars

195
00:06:39,400 --> 00:06:41,770
there over the next decade.

196
00:06:42,010 --> 00:06:44,502
Amazon also sells Claude to its cloud

197
00:06:44,502 --> 00:06:45,474
customers,

198
00:06:45,474 --> 00:06:48,067
and builds AI models of its own.

199
00:06:48,317 --> 00:06:50,397
Watch the money move.

200
00:06:50,457 --> 00:06:52,924
Amazon sends cash to Anthropic as an

201
00:06:52,924 --> 00:06:54,197
investment.

202
00:06:54,347 --> 00:06:56,874
Anthropic sends cash back to Amazon to

203
00:06:56,874 --> 00:06:58,229
pay for computing,

204
00:06:58,229 --> 00:07:00,897
and Amazon books that as sales.

205
00:07:01,047 --> 00:07:03,592
When Amazon sells Claude to its customers,

206
00:07:03,592 --> 00:07:05,047
it keeps a cut.

207
00:07:05,167 --> 00:07:07,427
And when Anthropic's value rises,

208
00:07:07,427 --> 00:07:10,517
the stake on Amazon's books rises too.

209
00:07:10,517 --> 00:07:12,813
Google runs a similar loop,

210
00:07:12,813 --> 00:07:14,078
as investor,

211
00:07:14,078 --> 00:07:16,226
cloud supplier and rival.

212
00:07:16,483 --> 00:07:18,683
Critics have a name for this.

213
00:07:18,683 --> 00:07:20,833
Circular financing.

214
00:07:20,893 --> 00:07:21,836
Their worry:

215
00:07:21,836 --> 00:07:24,081
when the same money travels around a

216
00:07:24,081 --> 00:07:24,508
loop,

217
00:07:24,508 --> 00:07:26,731
demand can look stronger than it is,

218
00:07:26,731 --> 00:07:28,976
and everyone in the loop looks healthier

219
00:07:28,976 --> 00:07:30,923
because of the others.

220
00:07:31,303 --> 00:07:33,474
A rival has argued that the way

221
00:07:33,474 --> 00:07:35,887
Anthropic counts cloud sales makes its

222
00:07:35,887 --> 00:07:37,693
revenue look bigger.

223
00:07:37,733 --> 00:07:40,154
Some warn the risk lands on ordinary

224
00:07:40,154 --> 00:07:41,099
investors,

225
00:07:41,099 --> 00:07:42,183
like June.

226
00:07:42,273 --> 00:07:44,769
And Anthropic's own filing warns these

227
00:07:44,769 --> 00:07:47,865
ties could create conflicts of interest.

228
00:07:48,117 --> 00:07:50,887
The other side has a case too.

229
00:07:51,137 --> 00:07:52,372
The cash is real,

230
00:07:52,372 --> 00:07:54,891
and most of Claude's paying customers are

231
00:07:54,891 --> 00:07:57,507
ordinary businesses.

232
00:07:57,657 --> 00:08:00,076
Counting cloud sales in full is a

233
00:08:00,076 --> 00:08:01,930
standard accounting choice,

234
00:08:01,930 --> 00:08:04,349
and both rivals say they follow the

235
00:08:04,349 --> 00:08:05,487
rules.

236
00:08:05,627 --> 00:08:08,284
Suppliers lending to customers is an old

237
00:08:08,284 --> 00:08:09,367
idea.

238
00:08:09,367 --> 00:08:11,657
Carmakers do it every day.

239
00:08:11,657 --> 00:08:13,587
And it's all disclosed.

240
00:08:13,587 --> 00:08:15,996
So the real question is how much

241
00:08:15,996 --> 00:08:18,706
growth would survive if the loop slowed

242
00:08:18,706 --> 00:08:19,608
down.

243
00:08:19,850 --> 00:08:22,050
And here's the twist.

244
00:08:22,290 --> 00:08:24,490
Remember the group that bought extra stock

245
00:08:24,490 --> 00:08:26,190
after the IPO?

246
00:08:26,370 --> 00:08:27,341
Google,

247
00:08:27,341 --> 00:08:28,311
Nvidia,

248
00:08:28,311 --> 00:08:28,947
AMD,

249
00:08:28,947 --> 00:08:29,917
Broadcom,

250
00:08:29,917 --> 00:08:30,888
Micron,

251
00:08:30,888 --> 00:08:34,500
Microsoft and the chipmaker TSMC.

252
00:08:34,720 --> 00:08:35,949
Most of them are,

253
00:08:35,949 --> 00:08:38,181
directly or indirectly,

254
00:08:38,181 --> 00:08:40,689
part of the supply chain Anthropic runs

255
00:08:40,689 --> 00:08:41,540
on.

256
00:08:41,980 --> 00:08:44,929
The investors are often the suppliers,

257
00:08:44,929 --> 00:08:46,823
and part of what they put in

258
00:08:46,823 --> 00:08:49,047
can come back as sales.

259
00:08:53,483 --> 00:08:55,443
So who got rich?

260
00:08:55,443 --> 00:08:57,772
Let's play detective with a hypothetical

261
00:08:57,772 --> 00:08:59,343
early investor.

262
00:08:59,553 --> 00:09:01,684
In Anthropic's first funding round,

263
00:09:01,684 --> 00:09:04,077
investors paid what works out today to

264
00:09:04,077 --> 00:09:06,353
about a quarter a share.

265
00:09:06,523 --> 00:09:08,453
A small bet back then is now

266
00:09:08,453 --> 00:09:10,983
worth hundreds of times more.

267
00:09:10,983 --> 00:09:12,261
On paper.

268
00:09:12,517 --> 00:09:16,067
The biggest paper winners are the giants.

269
00:09:16,067 --> 00:09:18,555
Amazon bought its stake in pieces over

270
00:09:18,555 --> 00:09:19,664
several years,

271
00:09:19,664 --> 00:09:22,152
and before the IPO it was already

272
00:09:22,152 --> 00:09:24,414
carrying it at many times what it

273
00:09:24,414 --> 00:09:24,844
paid,

274
00:09:24,844 --> 00:09:26,880
with much of that gain flowing into

275
00:09:26,880 --> 00:09:28,837
its reported profits.

276
00:09:28,987 --> 00:09:31,379
But a paper gain isn't cash until

277
00:09:31,379 --> 00:09:33,308
the shares are sold.

278
00:09:33,550 --> 00:09:35,420
Then there are the employees,

279
00:09:35,420 --> 00:09:38,104
many paid partly in stock options,

280
00:09:38,104 --> 00:09:40,273
the right to buy shares at an

281
00:09:40,273 --> 00:09:40,788
old,

282
00:09:40,788 --> 00:09:41,930
low price.

283
00:09:42,160 --> 00:09:43,971
Reports say more than four hundred of

284
00:09:43,971 --> 00:09:45,980
them are now worth a fortune.

285
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:47,342
On paper.

286
00:09:47,583 --> 00:09:50,653
And on paper is the key phrase.

287
00:09:50,653 --> 00:09:53,283
Most insiders can't sell yet.

288
00:09:53,353 --> 00:09:55,328
A lockup keeps most of their shares

289
00:09:55,328 --> 00:09:57,501
off the market for about six months,

290
00:09:57,501 --> 00:09:59,476
up to a year for the co-founders

291
00:09:59,476 --> 00:10:01,173
and biggest holders.

292
00:10:01,183 --> 00:10:02,734
When the locks open,

293
00:10:02,734 --> 00:10:05,101
a wave of sellers can arrive at

294
00:10:05,101 --> 00:10:05,964
once.

295
00:10:06,217 --> 00:10:07,497
And June?

296
00:10:07,497 --> 00:10:09,757
Her share has no lockup.

297
00:10:09,757 --> 00:10:11,727
But notice the difference.

298
00:10:11,757 --> 00:10:14,115
The earliest backers paid pocket change,

299
00:10:14,115 --> 00:10:15,044
years ago,

300
00:10:15,044 --> 00:10:17,787
when nobody knew if this would work.

301
00:10:17,987 --> 00:10:19,346
June paid full price,

302
00:10:19,346 --> 00:10:21,565
on the day the story was most

303
00:10:21,565 --> 00:10:22,977
exciting.

304
00:10:22,977 --> 00:10:24,267
Same share.

305
00:10:24,267 --> 00:10:26,145
Very different bet.

306
00:10:30,583 --> 00:10:31,114
So,

307
00:10:31,114 --> 00:10:32,793
is it worth it?

308
00:10:32,793 --> 00:10:34,473
One simple test.

309
00:10:34,473 --> 00:10:36,495
Compare the company's value at the first

310
00:10:36,495 --> 00:10:38,713
close with the pace of its sales.

311
00:10:38,903 --> 00:10:40,975
The stock is priced at about twenty

312
00:10:40,975 --> 00:10:43,233
times its yearly sales pace.

313
00:10:43,233 --> 00:10:44,563
Not its profits.

314
00:10:44,563 --> 00:10:46,037
Its sales.

315
00:10:46,283 --> 00:10:49,143
Let me draw what that price assumes.

316
00:10:49,193 --> 00:10:51,362
Picture Anthropic years from now,

317
00:10:51,362 --> 00:10:52,312
when growth slows,

318
00:10:52,312 --> 00:10:55,593
as it does for every big company.

319
00:10:55,873 --> 00:10:58,201
For today's price to look sensible then,

320
00:10:58,201 --> 00:11:00,123
it would need profits on the scale

321
00:11:00,123 --> 00:11:02,879
of the most profitable companies on Earth,

322
00:11:02,879 --> 00:11:04,333
year after year.

323
00:11:04,503 --> 00:11:05,933
Not impossible.

324
00:11:05,933 --> 00:11:07,701
Just a lot to assume on day

325
00:11:07,701 --> 00:11:08,522
one.

326
00:11:08,783 --> 00:11:11,270
Picture June's share at a fork in

327
00:11:11,270 --> 00:11:12,423
the road.

328
00:11:12,733 --> 00:11:15,280
Several roads lead away from here,

329
00:11:15,280 --> 00:11:17,853
and nobody knows the odds of any

330
00:11:17,853 --> 00:11:18,968
of them.

331
00:11:19,217 --> 00:11:20,452
On the bright road,

332
00:11:20,452 --> 00:11:24,327
AI becomes as basic as electricity.

333
00:11:24,467 --> 00:11:27,539
Anthropic keeps winning big customers,

334
00:11:27,539 --> 00:11:30,047
its computing costs keep falling,

335
00:11:30,047 --> 00:11:32,302
and those huge contracts turn out to

336
00:11:32,302 --> 00:11:33,747
be bargains.

337
00:11:33,917 --> 00:11:35,145
On that road,

338
00:11:35,145 --> 00:11:38,264
today's price could one day look cheap.

339
00:11:38,517 --> 00:11:40,105
On the middle road,

340
00:11:40,105 --> 00:11:41,424
AI keeps growing,

341
00:11:41,424 --> 00:11:44,437
but competition squeezes prices.

342
00:11:44,437 --> 00:11:46,684
Anthropic becomes a big,

343
00:11:46,684 --> 00:11:48,174
healthy company,

344
00:11:48,174 --> 00:11:49,663
and the stock does fine,

345
00:11:49,663 --> 00:11:50,396
slowly,

346
00:11:50,396 --> 00:11:52,415
because so much of the good news

347
00:11:52,415 --> 00:11:54,278
was paid for up front.

348
00:11:54,517 --> 00:11:56,209
And on the dark road,

349
00:11:56,209 --> 00:11:57,041
growth slows,

350
00:11:57,041 --> 00:11:59,019
cheaper models catch up,

351
00:11:59,019 --> 00:12:02,487
and the bills keep arriving anyway.

352
00:12:02,677 --> 00:12:03,993
Profits stay thin,

353
00:12:03,993 --> 00:12:06,385
the company needs more money,

354
00:12:06,385 --> 00:12:09,071
and every new share it sells makes

355
00:12:09,071 --> 00:12:11,015
June's slice smaller.

356
00:12:15,450 --> 00:12:16,187
Step back,

357
00:12:16,187 --> 00:12:19,330
and there's a pattern older than AI.

358
00:12:19,480 --> 00:12:20,671
In a gold rush,

359
00:12:20,671 --> 00:12:23,344
the steadiest money is often made selling

360
00:12:23,344 --> 00:12:24,430
shovels.

361
00:12:24,560 --> 00:12:25,014
Here,

362
00:12:25,014 --> 00:12:27,642
the shovel sellers are the chipmakers and

363
00:12:27,642 --> 00:12:28,720
the clouds.

364
00:12:28,770 --> 00:12:30,361
They get paid as long as the

365
00:12:30,361 --> 00:12:31,475
building goes on,

366
00:12:31,475 --> 00:12:33,521
whether or not any AI lab turns

367
00:12:33,521 --> 00:12:35,030
a big profit.

368
00:12:35,120 --> 00:12:36,645
Some get paid twice,

369
00:12:36,645 --> 00:12:39,735
as investors and as suppliers.

370
00:12:39,983 --> 00:12:40,129
So,

371
00:12:40,129 --> 00:12:41,113
what did Wall Street buy?

372
00:12:41,163 --> 00:12:44,170
A share of one of the fastest-growing

373
00:12:44,170 --> 00:12:46,847
companies ever measured,

374
00:12:46,847 --> 00:12:49,853
with real customers and real cash.

375
00:12:49,983 --> 00:12:52,310
Attached to a giant stack of promises,

376
00:12:52,310 --> 00:12:54,266
and to partners on every side of

377
00:12:54,266 --> 00:12:55,453
the table.

378
00:12:55,673 --> 00:12:57,333
That doesn't make it a bubble,

379
00:12:57,333 --> 00:12:58,773
or a sure thing.

380
00:12:58,813 --> 00:13:00,517
It's a bet on how long the

381
00:13:00,517 --> 00:13:01,223
growth lasts,

382
00:13:01,223 --> 00:13:03,414
at a price that assumes it lasts

383
00:13:03,414 --> 00:13:05,231
a very long time.

384
00:13:05,483 --> 00:13:07,540
Anthropic may become one of the most

385
00:13:07,540 --> 00:13:10,403
important companies of the next decade.

386
00:13:10,533 --> 00:13:12,981
But importance and investment returns are

387
00:13:12,981 --> 00:13:14,693
two different things.

388
00:13:14,843 --> 00:13:16,815
June hasn't decided what to do with

389
00:13:16,815 --> 00:13:17,853
her share.

390
00:13:18,013 --> 00:13:20,378
The question isn't whether AI will change

391
00:13:20,378 --> 00:13:21,503
the world.

392
00:13:21,533 --> 00:13:23,107
It's how much of that future has

393
00:13:23,107 --> 00:13:26,067
already been priced into a single stock.

394
00:13:26,317 --> 00:13:26,963
Next time,

395
00:13:26,963 --> 00:13:29,856
we follow the money behind another hidden

396
00:13:29,856 --> 00:13:32,237
link of the world economy.

397
00:13:32,277 --> 00:13:34,662
This is The Hidden Economics.
